Chapter: E1000AnegBudget
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `e1000-await-aneg` used to be bounded by a count of a million. On the ASUS
 2026-08-15 that count cost 92,892,733 us, because every iteration is a full
 MDIC transaction and a real MDIO bus answers in tens of microseconds where the
 model answers instantly. It is bounded by a duration now.

 WHAT THIS ARM CAN AND CANNOT REACH, said before the arms rather than after.
 The timeout branch itself is unreachable here: the e1000 model reports
 aneg-done on the first BMSR read, so `e1000-await-aneg` returns 1 before any
 deadline is consulted. Forcing it needs an emulator flag. What IS testable is
 the budget arithmetic, which is the half most likely to be silently wrong,
 because a duration derived from a rate is exactly where a multiply and a
 divide get transposed.

 THE PROPERTY IS THE SAME WALL TIME AT DIFFERENT RATES. That is the whole point
 of the change: a count gives different durations on different buses, a budget
 gives the same one. Both real HPET rates this project has measured are here --
 23999999 from the ASUS and 14318179 from the bed -- and both must yield three
 seconds.

 We say:

Section: Fixtures

  metal-hz : Integer = 23999999
  bed-hz : Integer = 14318179

  seconds-of : Integer -> Integer
  seconds-of (rate) = if rate <= 0 then (0 - 1) else (e1000-aneg-ticks rate) / rate

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("budget-ms=" & show e1000-aneg-ms)
    print-line-uni ("metal-ticks=" & show (e1000-aneg-ticks metal-hz))
    print-line-uni ("metal-seconds=" & show (seconds-of metal-hz))
    print-line-uni ("bed-ticks=" & show (e1000-aneg-ticks bed-hz))
    print-line-uni ("bed-seconds=" & show (seconds-of bed-hz))
    print-line-uni ("same-wall-time=" & show (seconds-of metal-hz == seconds-of bed-hz))
    print-line-uni ("zero-rate-ticks=" & show (e1000-aneg-ticks 0))
    print-line-uni ("fuel-still-a-bound=" & show (e1000-aneg-fuel > 0))
  end
